      "id": "delimiter-read-unread",
      "text": "Starting with unread \"ab\", ReadBytes('\\n') or ReadString('\\n') returns \"ab\" with io.EOF and consumes both bytes. UnreadRune then fails, but UnreadByte succeeds and makes \"b\" readable again. If the caller repeats the delimiter read before calling UnreadByte, the second read returns zero bytes with io.EOF and UnreadByte still restores \"b\". On a fresh zero-value Buffer, a delimiter read followed by UnreadByte also returns nil, but leaves the buffer empty. By comparison, reading \"ab\\n\" through the delimiter returns the delimiter with a nil error, and one UnreadByte restores it before a second UnreadByte fails.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 466,
        "fragment": "b.lastRead = opRead"
      },
      "question": "OPEN: Should delimiter reads permit UnreadByte after io.EOF, including when they return zero bytes, despite the documented exclusions for both cases?"
    },
    {
      "id": "grow-unread-order",
      "text": "Starting with eight ASCII bytes in a slice of capacity eight, consume six with Next(6) and the seventh with ReadRune. If Grow(1) comes next, it moves the eighth byte to index zero in the same backing array and preserves the last-read marker. UnreadRune then returns nil without restoring the seventh byte, so the next ReadRune returns the eighth. If UnreadRune comes before Grow(1), it restores the seventh byte, and Grow preserves both unread bytes. If there is already a byte of spare tail capacity, Grow(1) leaves the read offset in place and UnreadRune after Grow restores the seventh byte.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 418,
        "fragment": "if b.off >= int(b.lastRead) {"
      },
      "question": "OPEN: After Grow moves the unread contents, should UnreadRune restore the rune or return an error instead of reporting success without restoring it?"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-to-drain-and-retry",
      "text": "Starting with unread \"abc\" in a backing slice of length and capacity three, WriteTo calls the writer once. If the writer returns (1, err), WriteTo returns (1, err) and a retry sends only \"bc\". A short count with a nil error violates the io.Writer contract and produces io.ErrShortWrite with the same suffix retained. If the writer returns (3, nil), WriteTo resets the buffer, preserving capacity and making all three bytes available. If it returns (3, err), WriteTo returns (3, err) with Len and Available both zero. A subsequent WriteTo skips the writer, resets the buffer, and makes all three bytes available.",
      "citation": {
