    {
      "id": "read-from-eof-order",
      "text": "Starting with an empty Buffer backed by a slice of capacity MinRead, ReadFrom first gives the reader MinRead bytes of space. If the reader fills that slice and returns io.EOF with the data, ReadFrom retains the data and returns (MinRead, nil) at the original capacity. If the reader returns the same data with nil and reports io.EOF on the following call, ReadFrom allocates additional space before that call, then returns the same count and contents with greater capacity. A non-EOF error returned with data is propagated after those bytes are appended and counted. A reader that keeps returning (0, nil) leaves ReadFrom looping without progress.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 227,
        "fragment": "i := b.grow(MinRead)"
      },
      "question": "OPEN: Should the MinRead comment refer to the supplied reader's Read method, which receives the slice, instead of Buffer.Read?"
    },
    {
      "id": "peek-and-next",
      "text": "Starting with unread \"abc\", ReadByte returns 'a'. Peek(3) then returns an alias of \"bc\" with io.EOF, leaves Len at two, and allows UnreadByte to restore 'a'. Starting again from immediately after ReadByte, Peek(-1) panics and leaves that undo available after recovery. Next(-1) also panics, but clears the undo marker before the panic. Next(0) likewise clears the marker while returning an empty slice and leaving the read offset in place.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 359,
        "fragment": "b.lastRead = opInvalid"
      },
      "question": "OPEN: Should Peek and Next document the negative-count panic and whether those calls preserve a previous read's undo state?"
    },
    {
      "id": "borrowed-and-copied-bytes",
      "text": "Starting with NewBuffer(make([]byte, 0, 8)), append \"ab\\n\" to AvailableBuffer. Len remains zero until the immediately following Write of that slice, which returns (3, nil) and makes \"ab\\n\" readable. ReadBytes('\\n') returns \"ab\\n\" with nil error in independent storage. After Reset and WriteString(\"xy\\n\"), which reuses the buffer's backing array, the earlier ReadBytes result still contains \"ab\\n\". A fresh Bytes slice aliases the new contents, so changing its first byte to 'z' makes the next ReadByte return 'z'.",
